<section>
  <exercises>
    <heading level="1">9.4. Self-Check Exercise</heading>
    <exercise number="1" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Use Euler’s method with $$n = 5$$ to obtain approximations to the solution of the initial-value problem</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$y^{\prime} = 2x + y y(0) = 1$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>when $$x = 1$$ .</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Here, $$x_{0} = 0$$ and $$b = 1$$ so taking $$n = 5$$ , we find</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$h = \frac{1 - 0}{5} = \frac{1}{5}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>and</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{matrix} {x_{0} = 0} &amp; {x_{1} = \frac{1}{5}} &amp; {x_{2} = \frac{2}{5}} \\ {x_{3} = \frac{3}{5}} &amp; {x_{4} = \frac{4}{5}} &amp; {x_{5} = b = 1} \end{matrix}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Also,</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$F{({x,y})} = 2x + y$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>and</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$y_{0} = y{(0)} = 1$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Therefore, the approximations to the actual solution at the points $$x_{0},x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{5} = 1$$ are</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{lclcl} y_{0} &amp; = &amp; {y{(0)} = 1} &amp; &amp; \\ y_{1} &amp; = &amp; {y_{0} + hF{({x_{0},y_{0}})}} &amp; = &amp; {1 + \frac{1}{5}{({0 + 1})} = \frac{6}{5}} \\ y_{2} &amp; = &amp; {y_{1} + hF{({x_{1},y_{1}})}} &amp; = &amp; {\frac{6}{5} + \frac{1}{5}\left( {\frac{2}{5} + \frac{6}{5}} \right) = \frac{38}{25}} \\ y_{3} &amp; = &amp; {y_{2} + hF{({x_{2},y_{2}})}} &amp; = &amp; {\frac{38}{25} + \frac{1}{5}\left( {\frac{4}{5} + \frac{38}{25}} \right) = \frac{248}{125}} \\ y_{4} &amp; = &amp; {y_{3} + hF{({x_{3},y_{3}})}} &amp; = &amp; {\frac{248}{125} + \frac{1}{5}\left( {\frac{6}{5} + \frac{248}{125}} \right) = \frac{1638}{625}} \\ y_{5} &amp; = &amp; {y_{4} + hF{({x_{4},y_{4}})}} &amp; = &amp; {\frac{1638}{625} + \frac{1}{5}\left( {\frac{8}{5} + \frac{1638}{625}} \right) = \frac{10,828}{3125}} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Thus, the approximate value of $$y{(1)}$$ is</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\frac{10,828}{3125} \approx 3.4650$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
  <exercises>
    <heading level="1">9.4. Exercises</heading>
    <instruction>A calculator is recommended for this exercise set. In Exercises 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 and 10, use Euler’s method with (a) $$n = 4$$ and (b) $$n = 6$$ to obtain approximations to the solution of the initial-value problem when $$x = b$$ .</instruction>
    <exercise number="1" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y^{\prime} = x + y$$ , $$y(0) = 1$$ ; $$b = 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>$$y{(1)} = \frac{369}{128} \approx 2.8828$$</item>
          <item>$$y{(1)} = \frac{70,993}{23,328} \approx 3.043$$</item>
        </list>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="2" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y^{\prime} = x - 2y$$ , $$y(0) = 1$$ ; $$b = 2$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="3" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y^{\prime} = 2x - y + 1$$ , $$y(0) = 2$$ ; $$b = 2$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>$$y{(2)} = \frac{51}{16} \approx 3.1875$$</item>
          <item>$$y{(2)} = \frac{793}{243} \approx 3.2634$$</item>
        </list>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="4" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y^{\prime} = 2xy$$ , $$y(0) = 1$$ ; $$b = 0.5$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="5" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y^{\prime} = - 2\mathit{xy}^{2}$$ , $$y(0) = 1$$ ; $$b = 0.5$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>$$y{(0.5)} \approx 0.8324$$</item>
          <item>$$y{(0.5)} \approx 0.8207$$</item>
        </list>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="6" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y^{\prime} = x^{2} + y^{2}$$ , $$y(0) = 1$$ ; $$b = 1.5$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="7" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y^{\prime} = \sqrt{x + y}$$ , $$y(1) = 1$$ ; $$b = 1.5$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>$$y{(1.5)} \approx 1.7831$$</item>
          <item>$$y{(1.5)} \approx 1.7920$$</item>
        </list>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="8" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y^{\prime} = {({1 + x^{2}})}^{- 1}$$ , $$y(0) = 0$$ ; $$b = 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="9" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y^{\prime} = \frac{x}{y}$$ , $$y(0) = 1$$ ; $$b = 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>$$y{(1)} \approx 1.3390$$</item>
          <item>$$y{(1)} \approx 1.3654$$</item>
        </list>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="10" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y^{\prime} = \mathit{xy}^{1 \slash 3}$$ , $$y(0) = 1$$ ; $$b = 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <instruction>In Exercises 11, 12, 13, 14 and 15, use Euler’s method with $$n = 5$$ to obtain approximations to the solution to the initial-value problem over the indicated interval.</instruction>
    <exercise number="11" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y^{\prime} = \frac{1}{2}xy$$ , $$y(0) = 1$$ ; $$0 \leq x \leq 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>$$\mathbf{x}$$</cell>
              <cell>0.0</cell>
              <cell>0.2</cell>
              <cell>0.4</cell>
              <cell>0.6</cell>
              <cell>0.8</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>$$\mathbf{y}_{\mathbf{n}}$$</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
